Subject: [PATCH] widget/gtk: ask Wayland compositors to blur behind translucent window parts

Why Firefox source must change:
  Aequera's glass frame material (docs/design/MATERIAL.md) paints the chrome
  frame translucent and relies on the desktop to blur what shows through it.
  Hyprland blurs every translucent window, but KWin (Plasma 6.7+) blurs only
  surfaces that ask, through the staging protocol ext-background-effect-v1
  (wayland-protocols 1.45+), which Hyprland 0.56 also implements. Gecko does
  not speak it, and chrome code cannot reach a wl_surface, so on KDE the
  frame was either glass over a sharp wallpaper or forced solid.

What it changes:
  - widget/gtk/wayland: generated client code for ext-background-effect-v1
    (wayland-scanner 1.26.0, wayland-protocols 1.49), clang-formatted, with
    the visibility pragma the other protocol files carry.
  - nsWaylandDisplay binds ext_background_effect_manager_v1 and tracks its
    blur capability; a capability change after startup notifies
    LookAndFeel (media queries only).
  - nsWindowWayland::UpdateBackgroundBlur: for a mapped toplevel, sets the
    blur region of GTK's toplevel wl_surface to the client area minus the
    window's opaque region (the display list's, which the opaque-region path
    already computes) and minus the rounded CSD corners when they are drawn,
    then commits it (double-buffered state; GTK commits that surface only on
    decoration repaints, and the PiP code commits it the same way; our own
    subsurface is desynchronized). Runs on map, container resize, and opaque
    region changes; skips unchanged regions; cleared on unmap and destroy.
    An opaque (solid) frame leaves only slivers the opaque region misses, so
    it costs the compositor next to nothing.
  - Coordinates: surface-local as the protocol says (client area offset by
    the CSD shadow margins), except on KDE: KWin 6.7 places the region
    relative to the window geometry (src/plugins/blur/blur.cpp,
    BlurEffect::blurRegion translates by contentsRect().topLeft()), so there
    the margins are left out. Drop that branch once KWin follows the
    protocol. With tiled states (Hyprland) GTK has no margins and both agree.
  - pref widget.wayland.background-blur.enabled, default false, so
    Firefox's behavior is unchanged; aequera-prefs.js turns it on.

Depends on: nothing.
Validation: compiled Linux build (157.0.1, bootstrap toolchains). Aequera in
  KWin 6.7.5 nested in headless sway, XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=KDE: WAYLAND_DEBUG
  shows the capability, get_background_effect on the toplevel surface and
  set_blur_region with the client area minus the corners; screenshots show
  the checkerboard wallpaper evenly blurred behind the whole frame, sharp
  outside the rounded corners; switching to the solid frame shrinks the
  region to slivers under opaque pixels. Without the KDE branch KWin left a
  margin-wide unblurred band (the quirk above). Hyprland 0.56.2 nested in
  that KWin: frame blurred, tiled and floating. Mochitest gate (Aequera
  760/760 compiled, 758/758 artifact).
  Rebased onto 158.0: conflicted only with Bug 2070729 (ForceToplevelCommit
  and mCommitWindow added next to OnMapNative and the PiP members); both
  kept. The blur code still commits GTK's toplevel wl_surface itself, as
  PiP still does. Not yet compiled or run on 158.0 (no Linux build host).
Upstream risk: medium. Hooks nsWindow's opaque-region path, container
  size-allocate, and the Wayland map/unmap/destroy paths next to PiP's;
  conflicts if Mozilla reworks those. If Mozilla adopts the protocol
  itself, drop this patch in favor of upstream's.

